window.BS_DOCS = [{"t":"Quick start and first run","u":"guides/quick-start.html","d":"Install Bookmark Scope, pin it, see what is already saved for the site you are on, and open the dashboard. About ten minutes.","h":["1. Install and pin","2. Open the popup on a normal website","3. Change the scope","4. Bookmark or un-bookmark the page","5. Open the dashboard","6. Find your duplicates","7. Know how Undo works before you delete anything","What to try next"],"x":"What you will do: install the extension, pin it, use the popup on a normal web page, open the dashboard, and try one safe cleanup. Before you start: Chrome (the only browser this guide has been checked against) and a few existing bookmarks. You do not need to change any settings. 1. Install and pin # Add the extension from the Chrome Web Store (or follow Installation to load it unpacked). Then pin it so the toolbar button is always visible: Click the Extensions (puzzle piece) button in the Chrome toolbar. Find Bookmark Scope and click its pin icon. On a fresh install Bookmark Scope also shows a notification titled Pin Bookmark Scope for faster access , and a Quick setup card at the top of the dashboard. Choose Got it on the card to dismiss it. 2. Open the popup on a normal website # Go to any http or https page, then click the Bookmark Scope toolbar button. The popup answers one question: what do I already have saved for this site? You should see: the page address under the title, a pill such as 3 in scope , a search box, and a three-way Page / Host / Domain switch. The list shows up to eight matching bookmarks. On a page you have never saved the list is empty. On a browser-internal page (for example chrome://extensions ) the popup says Unsupported page and shows No scope . That is expected: Bookmark Scope only matches web addresses. 3. Change the scope # Choose Page , Host or Domain to widen or narrow what counts as &ldquo;the same site&rdquo;: Scope Matches Example for https://docs.example.com/guide Page The same page address Only bookmarks of that exact page Host The same host name Anything on docs.example.com Domain The same registrable domain (the default) Anything on example.com , including www.example.com and shop.example.com Your choice is remembered. The number shown on the toolbar icon follows the same scope. The popup guide explains each control. 4. Bookmark or un-bookmark the page # The star button in the popup header says Bookmark current page when the page is not saved, and Remove bookmark when it is. Removing a bookmark from the popup shows a toast with Undo . 5. Open the dashboard # Choose Dashboard in the popup header. The dashboard opens in a new tab and shows your whole bookmark library, not just one site. The dashboard: search and filters on top, your library in the middle, details for the active bookmark on the right. You should see: a header reading N total bookmarks in the library , a search box, filter chips ( All, Duplicates, Untitled, Old, Collisions ) with counts, and the list. Nothing has been changed or sent anywhere. 6. Find your duplicates # Filter Click the Duplicates chip. The list now shows only bookmarks whose page address appears more than once. Inspect Click a row. The detail panel shows its full address, folder, the date it was added and its age. Preview, do not delete yet Open Library tools and choose Duplicate preview . You pick which copy to keep in each group and see exactly what will be removed. Nothing changes until you press Merge and remove duplicates . Full details, including how tags are merged, are in Cleaning up . 7. Know how Undo works before you delete anything # After a delete, a toast shows how many bookmarks were deleted and an Undo button. Undo recreates the bookmarks in their folders and puts their tags back, but: the browser gives them new internal IDs and a new &ldquo;date added&rdquo; , because a bookmark cannot be recreated with its original ones; Undo only lives in the open dashboard or popup. If you close the tab, you cannot undo. Be careful Before a large cleanup, take a backup: Library tools &rarr; Export snapshot . See Backup and restore . What to try next # Tag bookmarks and filter by tag. Check for broken links . This is the one feature that contacts the sites you bookmarked, and it asks for your permission first. Learn the keyboard shortcuts , starting with Ctrl + Shift + P . Open settings to set your default scope, theme and language."},{"t":"Installation, updates and removal","u":"guides/installation.html","d":"Install Bookmark Scope from the Chrome Web Store or load it unpacked, pin it, update it, and remove it without losing your bookmarks.","h":["Requirements","Option A: Chrome Web Store","Option B: Load unpacked (from source)","Pinning and quick access","Updating"],"x":"Requirements # Chrome with Manifest V3 extension support. Chromium-based browsers share the extension format, but this guide and the project&rsquo;s recorded acceptance tests only cover Chromium/Chrome. Other browsers, including Firefox, are not verified. No account and no sign-in. Bookmark Scope has no server component. Option A: Chrome Web Store # Find it Open the Bookmark Scope &ndash; Domain & Page Manager listing in the Chrome Web Store. The project&rsquo;s GitHub repository is the other place to confirm you have the right extension. Add it Choose Add to Chrome and read Chrome&rsquo;s permission prompt before you confirm. The permissions Bookmark Scope declares are listed in Privacy, permissions and data . Pin it Open the Extensions menu (puzzle piece) and pin Bookmark Scope . Older releases This guide describes the current release. If Chrome&rsquo;s install prompt asks for access to all websites up front, you are looking at an older release that predates the optional site-access model described in Link health . Update to a current release to match this guide. Option B: Load unpacked (from source) # Use this to try a build from the public repository, or to review exactly what runs. Get the files Download the repository as a ZIP from GitHub and extract it, or clone it with Git: git clone https://github.com/ehsanenaloo/Bookmark-Scope.git Open the extensions page Go to chrome://extensions and turn on Developer mode (top right). Load the extension folder Choose Load unpacked and select the folder named extension inside the repository you just downloaded. It is the folder that directly contains manifest.json , popup.html and dashboard.html . Do not select the top folder of the repository. You should see: a card named Bookmark Scope &ndash; Domain & Page Manager with the version number and no error badge. If Chrome shows Manifest file is missing or unreadable , you most likely selected the top folder of the repository (or a folder inside extension ) instead of the extension folder itself. Note An unpacked copy gets its own extension ID, so it can be installed next to the Web Store version. The two do not share data: tags, saved views and settings are separate. An unpacked extension also depends on the folder staying where it is. If you move or delete the folder, Chrome disables the extension. Your bookmarks are never affected, but see Uninstalling and your data for what happens to Bookmark Scope&rsquo;s own data. Pinning and quick access # Pin the extension from the Extensions menu. Chrome also lets you assign a keyboard shortcut to an extension&rsquo;s toolbar button at chrome://extensions/shortcuts . That is a Chrome feature rather than something Bookmark Scope adds, and Bookmark Scope does not ship a default shortcut. Updating # How you installed How it updates Chrome Web Store Chrome updates it automatically in the background. To check now, open chrome://extensions , turn on Developer mode and use Update . Load unpacked Replace the folder contents (or run git pull ), then press the reload icon on the extension&rsquo;s card. Your data survives updates. On startup Bookmark Scope checks its stored data format (currently schema version 7), fills in defaults for anything new and keeps your tags, saved views, history and preferences. One deliberate exception: cached link-health results from before a format change may be discarded and are rebuilt the next time you run a scan. They are only a cache. Uninstalling and your data To remove the extension, open chrome://extensions and choose Remove . What After you uninstall Your bookmarks, folders and their order Unchanged. They belong to the browser, not to the extension. Bookmark Scope tags, saved views, link-health results, history, settings, recovery journals Removed. Chrome deletes an extension&rsquo;s stored data when it is uninstalled. Tags are stored by the extension, not in the bookmark itself, so they do not survive. Optional site access you granted for link checks Removed together with the extension. Before you uninstall If you want to keep your tags, export a snapshot first ( Library tools &rarr; Export snapshot ). A snapshot file includes your folder tree and tags. See Backup and restore . Clearing only some data Link-health cache: Library tools &rarr; Clear cached health data . Bookmarks are untouched. Preferences: Settings &rarr; Reset defaults restores nine preferences (see Settings reference ). It does not remove tags, saved views, history or scan settings. Site access: revoke it any time from the extension&rsquo;s Details page at chrome://extensions . The exact wording of Chrome&rsquo;s site-access controls is up to Chrome."},{"t":"The popup: scope, search and quick actions","u":"guides/popup.html","d":"How the Bookmark Scope popup works: page, host and domain scope, URL matching, search with fuzzy fallback, quick actions, the toolbar badge and the right-click menu.","h":["What the popup shows","Scope: page, host or domain","Searching in the popup","Quick actions on a bookmark","The toolbar badge","The right-click menu","Popup limits"],"x":"The popup on a page that already has several bookmarks. What the popup shows # Header The Bookmark Scope name and the address of the current tab. On a page that cannot be matched it says Unsupported page . Dashboard button Opens the library dashboard in a new tab. Star button Bookmark current page or Remove bookmark , depending on whether this exact address is already bookmarked. It is disabled on unsupported pages. More controls menu Open settings , Open Chrome bookmarks , About , plus the sort order, colour palette and theme controls. Status pills N in scope , and when relevant N old and N duplicate (duplicate groups). Hover a pill for its explanation. Search box and scope switch The search filters only the bookmarks in the current scope. The Page / Host / Domain switch changes the scope. List Up to eight bookmarks. If there are more, the popup says Showing 8 of N and points you to the dashboard. Scope: page, host or domain # The scope decides which saved bookmarks count as &ldquo;this site&rdquo;. The default is Domain ; change the default under Settings &rarr; Default mode . Switching scope in the popup is remembered. Mode Two bookmarks match when&hellip; Details Page Their normalised page address is identical Protocol, host (with a non-default port), path and, depending on your settings, query and fragment must agree. Host Their host name is identical docs.example.com and example.com are different hosts. Ports are ignored in host mode. Domain Their registrable domain is identical Computed with the Public Suffix List packaged inside the extension, so example.co.uk is treated as one site and two github.io project sites are not. How strictly a &ldquo;page&rdquo; is compared is controlled by two options, Ignore query string and Ignore hash fragment . The details and examples are in Finding and searching . Searching in the popup # Type in the search box. The popup first looks for an exact match: every word you type must appear somewhere in the bookmark&rsquo;s title, address or folder path, in any order, ignoring case. You can use the search operators too (for example is:duplicate ); see the operator table . If nothing matches and you used no operators , the popup falls back to fuzzy matching and says No exact matches &mdash; showing fuzzy results . Fuzzy matching accepts letters that appear in order but not next to each other (so ghb can find github.com ), and ranks results by relevance: title matches count most, then the address, then the folder path. Fuzzy results keep their relevance order and ignore the sort setting. Fuzzy matching is a popup feature. The dashboard search is exact (substring) matching. Quick actions on a bookmark # Open opens the address in a new tab. Show in bookmarks opens Chrome&rsquo;s bookmark manager on the bookmark&rsquo;s folder. Delete asks Delete bookmark? with Delete and Keep it . The result is a toast such as Deleted: 1. Failed: 0. with Undo . Each row can show badges: Duplicate , Old with an approximate age, Untitled and Title collision . What each means is explained in Cleaning up . Removing the page you are on The star looks filled only when a bookmark has exactly the same address text as the tab. If you saved the page with a different query string, fragment or trailing slash, the star stays empty and clicking it adds a second bookmark. If the star is filled, clicking it removes every bookmark whose normalised address matches the current tab (usually just one), then offers Undo. It does not ask first, so look at the toast. What popup Undo can and cannot do It recreates the removed bookmarks in the same folder and position where possible, and puts their tags back. It cannot restore the original browser ID or the original date added. If some bookmarks could not be removed, the toast reports both numbers ( Deleted and Failed ), and Undo covers only those that were actually removed. The Undo is kept only while the popup stays open. Closing the popup ends it. The toolbar badge # The number on the toolbar icon is the count of bookmarks in your popup scope for the active tab. It disappears when the count is zero or the page is unsupported, and is capped at 999. It updates when you switch tabs, finish loading a page, or change bookmarks. The right-click menu # Bookmark Scope adds three entries to Chrome&rsquo;s context menu: Where you right-click Menu entry Anywhere on a page Show all bookmarks of this domain Anywhere on a page Find duplicates of this page in bookmarks A link Find duplicates of this link in bookmarks Each entry opens the dashboard with a hint about the address you chose. The dashboard opens already narrowed to the address you chose: Show all bookmarks of this domain opens the dashboard in domain scope: only bookmarks on that registrable domain are listed. Find duplicates of this page/link in bookmarks opens it in page scope with the Duplicates filter on, so you see only bookmarks of that exact page that exist more than once. The scope is not shown as a chip. The count line (for example 3 shown &middot; 3 total ) reflects the narrowed list, and the header still says how many bookmarks the library has. To return to the whole library, open the dashboard again from the popup or reload it without the link. While you are in a scoped dashboard, the keys 1 , 2 and 3 switch between page, host and domain scope of that same address. Popup limits # The popup is scoped to the active tab&rsquo;s address. There is no way to browse the whole library from it, by design. It shows at most eight rows. Use the dashboard for the rest. The popup does not edit titles or tags. Use the dashboard. The popup sort control offers Title, Newest, Oldest and URL. A fifth option, Folder path, exists only as a default in Settings."},{"t":"Dashboard tour","u":"guides/dashboard-tour.html","d":"A guided tour of the Bookmark Scope dashboard: header, filters, side panels, health score, grouping, selection, the detail panel and the virtual list.","h":["Opening it","The header","The toolbar: search, grouping, filters, density","The side rail and its four panels","The list header","The list","The detail panel","The list is virtual","Narrow windows","Footer"],"x":"The dashboard at desktop width. Controls keep their labels as the visual design evolves. Opening it # From the popup, choose Dashboard . From the right-click menu entries (see the popup guide ), which open it already narrowed to a site. Unless it was opened from a right-click entry, the dashboard starts on your entire library . It loads your saved filters and grouping, restores cached link-health results (cache entries older than seven days are ignored) and refreshes itself when bookmarks change in Chrome, in the popup, or in another window. The header # The title and a count: N total bookmarks in the library . Buy me a coffee , which opens the project&rsquo;s support page. The More actions menu: About , Open Chrome bookmarks , Settings , and theme and colour-palette controls. On first run a Quick setup card explains how to pin the extension. Got it dismisses it permanently. The toolbar: search, grouping, filters, density # Search box Placeholder Search title, URL, or folder . Supports operators; see Finding and searching . Press / to jump to it. Grouping selector Flat view , Group by domain , Group by folder , Group by status . Sort order Next to the grouping selector (accessible name Sort visible bookmarks ): Title , URL , Newest , Oldest , Folder path . It orders the flat list, or the bookmarks inside each group. The choice is remembered and saved views include it. Group order Appears only when grouped and orders the groups themselves: Default order , Largest first , A &rarr; Z . Filter chips All , Duplicates , Untitled , Old , Collisions , each with a count for the whole library. One chip is active at a time. Density toggle Switch to compact density and back. It affects the current session only; it is not saved. The side rail and its four panels # A narrow rail on the side has four buttons, Stats , Clean , Health and Activity , and a collapse button. By default the rail is collapsed to icons and a panel opens as a flyout over the list when you click a button. Your collapse choice is saved. On wide windows you can expand it so the panel sits beside the list. Panel What it shows What you can do Stats (Overview) Library size, health score, number of checked links, a &ldquo;Priority&rdquo; count (broken, server-error, unreachable and redirected links), and up to two recommendations Click a recommendation button, for example Review duplicates or Inspect visible links Clean (Cleanup) Counts of duplicate URLs, old and untitled bookmarks Quick filters: Duplicates , Old , Untitled , Collisions Health How many links are checked, redirected and unhealthy, and the next review time Inspect visible (or Inspect selected ), Mark reviewed now Activity Recent actions: count of actions, redirects fixed, imports, deletes, and the five latest entries Read only The health score The score is a quick, rough indicator, not a verdict. It starts at 100 and subtracts capped penalties for the share of problems in the current library or scope: Problem Largest possible deduction Broken, server-error or unreachable links (among links you have checked) 35 Redirected links (among links you have checked) 12 Duplicate groups 18 Old bookmarks 16 Untitled bookmarks 10 Title collisions 9 Grades: A is 90 or above, B is 80&ndash;89, C is 70&ndash;79, D is 55&ndash;69 and F is below 55. Until you have checked links, the score is labelled metadata-only , because it can only judge duplicates, age and titles. The footer shows the same score. Activity history Bookmark Scope keeps a short log of its own actions (health scans, deletes, undo, redirect repairs, moves, mark-reviewed, clearing the cache and bookmarking the current page). It keeps the latest 60 entries. This is a local log, not a backup of your bookmarks. The list header # A summary such as 12 shown &middot; 480 total and the cleanup counts for the view. Select all / Clear all . Inspect visible ( Inspect selected when something is selected). See Link health . A More actions menu: Export visible (JSON), Export visible as CSV , Import bookmarks&hellip; , group expand/collapse items when grouped, and Clear cached health data . Library tools , which opens the maintenance dialog (snapshots, duplicate preview, saved views, bulk tags, resumable scan, import, diagnostics). If a scan is paused, a Saved scan progress button appears next to it. Library tools groups the maintenance commands. The list # Each row shows a favicon, the title, the host, the folder path (when it is not just the root), the full address, badges for cleanup issues and link health, any tags, and three buttons: Open bookmark , Show in bookmarks and More actions ( Edit , Repair redirect , Delete ). The list follows the Sort visible bookmarks control in the toolbar, which defaults to title A&ndash;Z and is kept between visits. The popup has its own sort setting (see Popup ); the two are independent. Grouping By domain uses the registrable domain ( example.com ). By folder uses the full folder path. By status puts each bookmark in the first category that applies, in this order: Duplicates , Old bookmarks , Untitled , Title collisions , Clean bookmarks . It groups cleanup status, not link health. Each group header has a collapse toggle, a count, small badges (duplicates, unhealthy, redirected, old), and the buttons Select group , Inspect this group and Open group . Be careful Open group opens every bookmark in the group as a new tab, with no confirmation. Check the count first. Selection Tick a row&rsquo;s checkbox, press Space on the active row, use Select all , or press Ctrl + A ( Cmd + A on Mac). Select all always means every bookmark in the filtered view , not only the rows currently drawn on screen. With rows selected, a bar offers batch actions. Library tools stays available. The selection bar shows N selected and icon buttons (hover or listen to their names) Clear all , Open selected , Inspect selected , Copy URLs , Repair redirected (only enabled when a selected bookmark has a repairable redirect) and Delete . Tools that act on &ldquo;selected or visible&rdquo; bookmarks, such as Bulk tags and Resumable scan, use the selection when there is one and otherwise use everything visible, and say which in the dialog. The detail panel # Click a row, or move with the arrow keys, to make it active. The detail panel shows the active bookmark."},{"t":"Finding and searching","u":"guides/finding-and-searching.html","d":"Search operators, filters, tag chips, fuzzy fallback, and exactly how Bookmark Scope decides that two URLs are the same page, host or domain.","h":["Search basics","Operators","Filter chips","Tag chips","Fuzzy search (popup only)"],"x":"Search basics # Type in the dashboard search box (press / to focus it). Results update as you type. The text is split on spaces. Every word must match (AND). A plain word matches if it appears in the bookmark&rsquo;s title, address or folder path . Matching ignores case and is a substring match, so git matches github.com . Word order does not matter. Search works on the current view only: the dashboard&rsquo;s whole library, narrowed by the active filter chip and any selected tags. Operators # Add an operator to a word to look in one place only. There is no space after the colon. Operator Matches Example title: The title contains the text title:invoice url: The full address contains the text url:/blog/ path: The folder path contains the text path:reading host: The host name contains the text host:docs. domain: The registrable domain contains the text domain:example health: The link-health status equals the value. Values: healthy , redirected , broken , server-error , unreachable , unknown , plus the shortcuts bad (broken, server-error or unreachable) and good or ok (healthy or redirected) health:bad is: A cleanup flag: duplicate , untitled , old , collision (or title-collision ) is:duplicate age: age:old or age:stale : older than 730 days age:old Combine them freely, for example domain:example is:duplicate health:bad or path:reading title:guide . What search does not do There are no quoted phrases. \"exact phrase\" is treated as separate words, quote marks included. There is no tag: operator. Use the tag chips (below). An operator with no text after it ( title: ) is ignored, and unknown is: values have no effect. health: only matches bookmarks that already have a recorded link-health result. Until you run a scan, nothing is &ldquo;broken&rdquo; or &ldquo;healthy&rdquo; (see Link health ). The dashboard search is exact. Fuzzy matching is a popup feature (see below). Filter chips # The chips under the search box apply a cleanup filter on top of your search. Only one is active at a time, and the choice is remembered between visits. Chip Keeps bookmarks that&hellip; All No cleanup filter Duplicates Share a page address with at least one other bookmark Untitled Have no real title (empty, untitled , new bookmark or (Untitled bookmark) ) Old Were added 730 or more days ago Collisions Use a title that is also used for a different address The number on each chip counts the whole library. Press Esc (with the cursor outside the search box) to clear the search text first, then the active chip. See the Escape ladder . Ordering results # Search narrows the list; it does not rank it. In the dashboard, the Sort visible bookmarks control beside the grouping selector orders what is left by title, address, newest, oldest or folder path (see Dashboard tour ). Tag chips # When at least one bookmark has a tag, a Tags: strip appears above the list. It shows your 12 most-used tags plus any tag you have switched on. Click a chip to require that tag; click again to remove it. With several tags on, a bookmark must have all of them. Clear removes the tag filter. Clicking a tag on a row does the same as clicking its chip. Tags are described in Organizing . Fuzzy search (popup only) # If the popup finds no exact match and your text has no operators, it ranks approximate matches: an exact substring scores highest, then letters that appear in order. Title matches are weighted most, then address, then folder. A line reading No exact matches &mdash; showing fuzzy results tells you it happened. The dashboard never switches to fuzzy matching, so an empty dashboard result means no exact match. How Bookmark Scope decides two URLs are the same Duplicate detection, the popup&rsquo;s page scope, the toolbar badge, link-health result keys and the duplicate preview all use one definition of a &ldquo;page&rdquo;: Only http and https addresses are understood. Other schemes ( file: , chrome: , javascript: ) are never matched or checked. The host name is lower-cased. The path is kept case-sensitive . A trailing slash is dropped ( /docs/ equals /docs ). http and https are different pages. A non-default port is part of the identity ( :8443 ); a default port such as :443 on https is not. The query string ( ?a=1 ) is kept by default. Turn on Ignore query string in page matching to drop it. The fragment ( #section ) is ignored by default. Turn off Ignore hash fragment in page matching to keep it. Both options live in Settings . Changing them re-evaluates every duplicate count and the popup scope. Examples Each row compares two addresses using the default options (query kept, fragment ignored). Same page? is what the Duplicates filter and Page scope use. Verified by running the extension&rsquo;s URL functions. Address A Address B Same page? Same host? Same domain? https://example.com/docs https://example.com/docs/ Yes Yes Yes https://example.com/docs https://EXAMPLE.com/docs Yes Yes Yes https://example.com/docs https://example.com:443/docs Yes Yes Yes https://example.com/docs http://example.com/docs No Yes Yes https://example.com/docs https://example.com:8443/docs No Yes Yes https://example.com/docs https://example.com/Docs No Yes Yes https://example.com/docs https://www.example.com/docs No No Yes https://example.com/docs?a=1 https://example.com/docs?a=2 No (Yes if query is ignored) Yes Yes https://example.com/docs#top https://example.com/docs#end Yes (No if fragments are kept) Yes Yes https://docs.example.co.uk/a https://shop.example.co.uk/b No No Yes https://alice.github.io/a https://bob.github.io/b No No No The last two rows show the Public Suffix List at work: co.uk is a public suffix, so example.co.uk is one site; github.io is also listed as a suffix, so every *.github.io project is its own domain. Note Titles play no part in duplicate detection. Two bookmarks with different titles but the same page address are duplicates. Two bookmarks with the same title and different addresses are a title collision , which is a separate, softer signal."},{"t":"Organizing: tags, folders and moving","u":"guides/organizing.html","d":"Tag bookmarks, bulk-change tags with a preview and undo, move bookmarks with drag and drop, and understand what Bookmark Scope can and cannot do with folders.","h":["Tags in one minute","Adding tags","Finding by tag","Editing a bookmark","Moving bookmarks (drag and drop)","Folders"],"x":"Tags in one minute # A tag is a short label such as work or read later . A bookmark can have several. Tags are normalised: lower-cased, trimmed, with runs of spaces collapsed, and unprintable characters removed. Work , work and WORK become the same tag. A tag can be up to 32 characters , and a bookmark can have up to 20 tags. Tags belong to the bookmark&rsquo;s browser ID. They follow a bookmark when you move or rename it, and are removed when you delete it. Tags are stored by the extension on this browser profile only. Chrome does not store extra fields on a bookmark, and Bookmark Scope does not sync tags between devices. They are included in snapshots and in the JSON export (see Backup and restore ). Adding tags # Bulk tags (works on many bookmarks at once) Bulk tags: choose an operation, preview the change, then apply. Choose the targets Select specific bookmarks, or leave nothing selected to target everything currently visible (after your search, chip and tag filters). The dialog names its scope, for example Visible bookmarks (current filters): 42 , so check the number before continuing. Open the tool Choose Library tools &rarr; Bulk tags , or open the command palette and type bulk . Pick the operation Add tag (one field), Remove tag (one field), Rename tag and Merge tag (a source and a target field). Rename and Merge both replace the source tag with the target tag on the targeted bookmarks; use Merge when the target tag already exists elsewhere. Preview Choose Preview . You get Bookmarks to change: N and, for each bookmark, Before and After . Changing any field clears the preview so you cannot apply a stale one. A change that would exceed 20 tags on any bookmark is refused as a whole, with no partial changes. Apply Choose Apply tag changes . The result reads Changed / missing / conflicting with three numbers. Missing means a bookmark was deleted meanwhile; conflicting means its tags changed after the preview and it was left alone. Undo for bulk tags Undo tag changes sits inside the same dialog and reverses only the bookmarks whose tags still match the result you applied. If you or another window changed them since, they are reported as conflicts and not overwritten. This Undo lives in the open dashboard session and is gone after a reload. Tag a whole site in two steps Search domain:example so only that site is visible. Open Bulk tags, choose Add tag , type the tag, preview, apply. The inline tag box on a row Click a row to make it active; it then shows a dashed + add tag box. Type a tag and press Enter or , to add it; Esc clears the box. Each tag on a row has a small &times; to remove it. Clicking a tag&rsquo;s name adds it to the tag filter. The same normalisation and limits apply (lower case, 32 characters, 20 per bookmark), and an invalid or empty tag shows a short error toast. Finding by tag # Use the Tags: chips above the list (see Tag chips ). Selected chips combine with AND. Tag chips and the search box work together. Editing a bookmark # Select the row and press E , or use Edit in the detail panel or the row&rsquo;s More actions menu. Change the title or address and save. The bookmark keeps its ID, folder and position. Moving bookmarks (drag and drop) # Drag a row and drop it on another row. Drop on the upper half of the target to insert before it, or the lower half to insert after . If the target is in a different folder, the bookmark moves into that folder at that position. If the dragged row is part of a multi-selection, all selected bookmarks move together, keeping their relative order. The toast reads Moved: N. Failed / not attempted: M. with Undo . Moves are applied one at a time. If one fails, the rest are not attempted and nothing is rolled back automatically. Undo restores only the moves that succeeded, and tells you if it could not complete. You cannot drop onto a bookmark you are dragging. Moving changes folder and position only; the bookmark keeps its ID, date and tags. How this was checked Drag and drop is described from the source and the project&rsquo;s recorded acceptance notes. It relies on the browser&rsquo;s desktop drag-and-drop; touch devices are not covered. Folders # Bookmark Scope can show each bookmark&rsquo;s folder, group by folder, filter by folder path and move bookmarks between folders by dropping. It does not create, rename, reorder or delete folders. Use Chrome&rsquo;s own bookmark manager for that: Press Show in bookmarks (on a row) or Show folder (in the detail panel) to open the manager on that folder. Or choose Open Chrome bookmarks from the header menu. When you restore a snapshot, Bookmark Scope does create a new folder to hold the restored tree; see Backup and restore ."},{"t":"Cleaning up: duplicates, untitled, old and collisions","u":"guides/cleaning-up.html","d":"How Bookmark Scope finds duplicates, untitled, old and title-colliding bookmarks, the duplicate preview with survivor choice, and exactly what delete and Undo can and cannot restore.","h":["What counts as clutter","Merging duplicates safely","Deleting bookmarks","Fixing untitled and vague titles","Reviewing old bookmarks","Repairing redirected links","A sensible cleanup routine"],"x":"What counts as clutter # Category Definition Good to know Duplicate Two or more bookmarks with the same normalised page address (see URL identity ) Every copy is flagged, including the one you would keep. Untitled The title is empty, or is untitled , new bookmark or (Untitled bookmark) Rows show the placeholder (Untitled bookmark) . Old Added 730 days (about two years) or more ago Uses the date Chrome recorded. The threshold is fixed. Title collision The same title (ignoring case and surrounding spaces) is used for more than one different page address Often fine. Useful for spotting vague titles such as Home . Use the filter chips, the Clean panel, or the operators is:duplicate , is:untitled , age:old , is:collision to see them. The Stats panel also turns the counts into suggestions such as Merge duplicate groups or Fix muddy metadata . Merging duplicates safely # Do not delete duplicates by hand. Use the preview, which lets you choose the survivor and shows what will happen. Narrow the view The preview acts on visible bookmarks. Clear unrelated filters or apply the Duplicates chip. The dialog states Visible bookmarks (current filters): N . Open the preview Choose Library tools &rarr; Duplicate preview . If there is nothing to merge you will see a message instead. Choose a survivor for each group Every group lists its copies with title, address, folder, date added and tags. A radio button marks the survivor. The default follows Settings &rarr; Merge strategy ( Keep newest bookmark by default, or Keep oldest bookmark ). You can override each group. Check the numbers The dialog shows Duplicate groups and Bookmarks to remove , and for each group the Merge tags result. Merge Choose Merge and remove duplicates . The preview is the confirmation, so there is no second prompt. What the merge does It re-checks everything first. If a bookmark was edited, moved or deleted since you opened the preview, the merge stops with Preview changed; reopen it before merging and changes nothing. The union of all copies&rsquo; tags is saved on the survivor before any copy is deleted. If that union would exceed 20 tags, the merge is refused up front. The survivor keeps its own title, address, folder, ID and date. The other copies are deleted with the normal delete flow below, including Undo. Undo after a merge brings the deleted copies back, but the survivor keeps the extra tags it gained. Deleting bookmarks # Select bookmarks and press Delete (the selection bar button, the detail panel button, the row menu, or Delete / Backspace with rows selected). A dialog asks Delete N bookmarks? with Delete and Keep them . The result is a toast: Deleted: N. Failed: M. with an Undo button. The toast stays until you click it, replace it with another action, or press Esc . What Undo restores The bookmark&rsquo;s title and address. Its folder, and its position among the surviving neighbours in that folder. Its tags, attached to the new bookmark. What Undo cannot restore The original ID and the original &ldquo;date added.&rdquo; Chrome assigns new ones. After Undo a bookmark may look new, and the Old filter will no longer count it as old. Anything stored by Chrome that extensions cannot set. A permanent safety net. The undo information lives in memory in the open dashboard. Reloading or closing the tab discards it. It is not a recycle bin. Partial failures A delete is not atomic. If some bookmarks cannot be removed (for example, one was already deleted elsewhere), the toast reports both numbers and Undo covers only the ones that were actually removed. Undo itself can partly fail; it then reports how many remain and lets you press Undo again to retry only those. Be careful For big cleanups, export a snapshot first ( Library tools &rarr; Export snapshot ). A snapshot is the only recovery that survives closing the tab. See Backup and restore . Fixing untitled and vague titles # Filter by Untitled or Collisions , open the detail panel and use Edit to give each bookmark a meaningful title. Editing keeps ID, folder and tags. Reviewing old bookmarks # Old does not mean dead. Combine the Old chip with a link-health scan ( age:old health:bad ) to find old bookmarks whose pages are also gone. Repairing redirected links # After a link-health scan, bookmarks that redirect to a different final address can be updated in place. This has its own rules and no Undo ; read Repairing redirects first. A sensible cleanup routine # Export a snapshot. Merge duplicates with the preview. Run a link check on what remains, review health:bad , and delete only what you have confirmed is dead. Give untitled bookmarks real titles. Repair redirects you have checked."},{"t":"Link health: checking, scanning and repairing","u":"guides/link-health.html","d":"How Bookmark Scope checks whether bookmarked pages still load: statuses, the optional site-access permission, manual and resumable scans, scheduled background scans, redirect repair and the cache.","h":["The short version","What the statuses mean","The permission model","Running a check from the dashboard","Resumable scans","The health cache","Practical tips"],"x":"The short version # A check sends a lightweight request to each bookmarked address from your own browser and records what came back. You must grant Chrome&rsquo;s optional site access for http and https pages first. If you say no, nothing is requested. Results are kept in a local cache for seven days. A result is a hint, not a verdict. Sites block bots, require sign-in and rate-limit, which can look like &ldquo;broken&rdquo;. What the statuses mean # Status shown When it is assigned What it usually means Healthy The server answered with a success status and no change of address The page loaded. This does not prove the content is the same as when you saved it. Redirected The request was redirected, or ended on a different page than the one you saved (differences in the #fragment are ignored) The page moved. You may be able to repair the bookmark . A different #fragment or host letter case alone does not count as a move. Broken HTTP status 400&ndash;499, shown with the code Not found (404), gone (410) &mdash; but also sign-in required (401), forbidden (403) and rate-limited (429). Check before deleting. Server error HTTP status 500 or higher The site had a problem. Often temporary. Unreachable / Timed out No response: DNS failure, refused connection, no network, or no answer in 8 seconds The site may be gone, or you may be offline. Unchecked No result recorded yet Run a check. Check failed (skipped) The request was skipped by policy, aborted, or produced no response Chrome Web Store addresses and non-web addresses are skipped. How the check works The extension asks for the page&rsquo;s headers with a HEAD request, following redirects and bypassing the HTTP cache. If the server answers 405 Method Not Allowed , it tries a GET instead, with a 5-second limit. The normal limit is 8 seconds per address. The classification looks at the status code and whether the final address changed. It does not read or judge the page content. The permission model # Bookmark Scope declares http://*/* and https://*/* as optional host permissions. They are not granted when you install. You start a check Choose Inspect visible , Inspect selected , Inspect this group , or start a resumable scan. Chrome asks If access has not been granted, Chrome shows its own prompt for site access. Read it. Bookmark Scope needs the broad pattern because it contacts whichever sites you bookmarked. You decide If you decline, you get Health scans need temporary site access. Permission denied, so the scan was not run. and no request is made. Revoking access The prompt wording says &ldquo;temporary&rdquo;, but Bookmark Scope does not revoke the permission for you afterwards. To remove it, open chrome://extensions , choose Details for Bookmark Scope and change its site access there. Chrome controls those screens and their exact wording. Enabling the scheduled scan in Settings also asks for the same permission when you save. Running a check from the dashboard # Choose what to check The check covers your selected bookmarks if any are selected; otherwise everything visible after filters. Duplicate addresses are checked once. Press Inspect The button reads Inspect visible or Inspect selected . While it runs it shows Stop inspect 12/80 . Up to four addresses are checked at a time. Watch the results appear Rows gain a health badge (for example Healthy 200 or Broken 404 ). Hover a badge for a redirect target or an error note. The Health panel and the score update as results arrive. Stop any time Press the same button again. Results already received are kept: Inspection stopped after 12/80 links&hellip; Partial results were kept. When a run finishes you get a summary such as Health scan finished for Entire library: 70 healthy &middot; 3 redirected &middot; 4 broken &middot; 1 server &middot; 2 unreachable. A scan is recorded in the Activity panel. To find what a scan flagged, search health:bad (broken, server error or unreachable), health:redirected or health:good . Resumable scans # For long lists, use Library tools &rarr; Resumable scan . It keeps its progress in storage so closing the page does not lose it. The dialog states its scope (selected or visible) and Unique supported URLs to check . Start new scan creates the job and begins. If an unfinished job already exists, tick Starting a new scan replaces the previous progress first. Pause scan , Resume scan and Cancel scan do what they say. A canceled job cannot be resumed; start a new one. The progress line reads Completed / total / remaining followed by a status: No saved scan , Ready to start , Scanning , Paused , Canceled or Completed . Addresses are checked one at a time. A job can hold between 1 and 50,000 addresses, and only one job is saved. It is paused, not running in the background A resumable scan only runs while its dashboard tab is open. Closing the dialog, or the tab, pauses it. Reopen Library tools ; a Saved scan progress button shows how far it got, and Resume scan continues. If a request was in flight when it stopped, that address may be checked again, but it is never counted twice. Scheduled background scans If you want link checks without opening the dashboard, enable the scheduled scan in Settings . It is off by default . Enabling it prompts for site access; if you decline it stays off. The interval is 1&ndash;90 days (default 7). It is driven by a Chrome alarm, so it only runs while the browser is running, and timing is approximate. Each run checks up to 250 bookmarks, one at a time. It remembers where it stopped and continues from there next time, so over several runs it works through the whole library instead of re-checking the same first 250. If, after a run, some bookmarks have newly turned broken, server-error or unreachable, you get one notification: Bookmark Scope: new broken links found &mdash; N bookmarks now appear broken. Open the dashboard to review and clean up. Settings shows Last scan: &hellip;. N bookmarks currently look broken. This number counts every "},{"t":"Review reminders and notifications","u":"guides/review-reminders.html","d":"Every notification Bookmark Scope can show, what triggers it, and how to set up review reminders.","h":["All notifications","Review reminders: what they are for","Turn them on","Mark reviewed now","If you also want link checks"],"x":"All notifications # Notification When it appears How to turn it off Pin Bookmark Scope for faster access On a fresh install, and again at browser start while the pin hint has not been dismissed Choose Got it on the Quick setup card in the dashboard or popup Bookmark Scope: new broken links found After a scheduled background scan finds bookmarks that newly look broken Turn off the scheduled scan in Settings Bookmark Scope review is due When a review reminder is enabled and its next date has passed Untick Enable review reminders in Settings Notifications use Chrome&rsquo;s notification system, so your operating system&rsquo;s focus or do-not-disturb settings can hide them. That is operating-system behavior, not something Bookmark Scope controls. Review reminders: what they are for # A review reminder is a gentle nudge to open the dashboard every N days. It only shows a notification, titled Bookmark Scope review is due , that mentions the date the review was scheduled for. It does not scan, change or delete anything, and it needs no site access. Turn them on # Open Settings Use the popup&rsquo;s More controls menu or the dashboard&rsquo;s More actions menu and choose Settings . Find the Review reminders section Tick Enable review reminders (off by default) and set Reminder interval (days) , from 1 to 365 (default 14). Save Choose Save settings . What you do What happens to the schedule Enable reminders for the first time The first reminder is scheduled interval days after your last recorded review, or after now if you have never recorded one. If that moment has already passed, it is set interval days from now Save again without changing the interval The schedule does not move Change the interval and save The schedule is re-anchored: last review date plus the new interval (or from now if that is already past) Untick the box and save Reminders stop and the pending alarm is cleared A reminder fires You get the notification, and the next one is scheduled one interval later A value outside 1&ndash;365 is corrected when you save. The background worker re-arms its timer whenever these settings change, and reminders can only fire while the browser is running. Mark reviewed now # Open the dashboard&rsquo;s Health panel and choose Mark reviewed now after you have gone through your library. It records the review in the Activity log. If reminders are on, it also moves the next reminder to one interval from now and shows Review marked complete. Next reminder &hellip; . If reminders are off it only logs the review. The dashboard footer and the Health panel show the state: reminders off , Next review in N days or Review due . When a review is due, the Stats panel also offers a Scheduled review is due recommendation. If you also want link checks # Use the scheduled background scan . It is separate: it needs site access, runs on its own timer and notifies you only when it finds something new."},{"t":"Smart views","u":"guides/smart-views.html","d":"Save a search, filter, grouping and tag selection as a named view you can reopen with one click. Views store the recipe, never the bookmarks.","h":["What a view stores","Create a view","Open, update or delete a view","Good recipes"],"x":"What a view stores # The search text (up to 2,000 characters). The cleanup filter chip ( All, Duplicates, Untitled, Old, Collisions ). The tag filter (tags you had switched on). The sort order, the grouping and the group order. It does not store bookmark titles, addresses, IDs or the selection. Views are live: if you add three new bookmarks that match a saved recipe, they appear next time you open it. You can keep up to 50 views. Names can be up to 80 characters. Create a view # Set the dashboard up For example search domain:example , choose the Old chip and Group by folder . Open the dialog Library tools &rarr; Saved views . Name and save Type a View name and choose Save view . The dialog confirms with View saved and keeps that view selected, so saving again updates it rather than creating a duplicate. Open, update or delete a view # Pick it in the Saved views list, then choose Open view . The dashboard applies the recipe, returns to the entire library and clears any selection. To change a view, open it, adjust the dashboard, reopen Saved views , select the same view and press Save view . Delete view removes it (the bookmarks are unaffected). Open view and Delete view stay disabled until a real saved view is selected. Good recipes # Goal Search Chip Grouping Old and dead health:bad Old Group by domain Clean-up queue Duplicates Group by domain Needs a title Untitled Flat view One project path:project-x All Group by folder Note Views live in this browser profile&rsquo;s extension storage. They are not included in a snapshot, and they do not sync between devices."},{"t":"Backup, restore, import and export","u":"guides/backup-and-restore.html","d":"Snapshots, import (browser bookmarks.html files, Pocket, Pinboard, Raindrop, the built-in JSON and CSV formats and plain URL lists), export as JSON or CSV, recovery journals, and what cannot be restored.","h":["Which tool for which job","Snapshots","Import","Export visible (JSON or CSV)","A backup routine that works"],"x":"Which tool for which job # I want to&hellip; Use Keeps Back up my folder tree and tags before a cleanup Export snapshot Folders, order, titles, addresses, dates (descriptive), tags Put a backup back Restore snapshot Recreates the tree in a new folder with new IDs Bring in bookmarks from a browser bookmarks.html file, Pocket, Pinboard, Raindrop or a text list Import bookmarks&hellip; Titles, addresses, tags, descriptive dates Hand a list of what I see to a spreadsheet or script Export visible (JSON) or Export visible as CSV Title, address, folder path, date, tags Backup and import commands live in Library tools. Snapshots # Export a snapshot Choose Library tools &rarr; Export snapshot . Your browser downloads bookmark-scope-snapshot.json . It records the full nested folder tree in order, with each bookmark&rsquo;s title, address, date added and tags, plus a creation time and the extension version. Format name: bookmark-scope-snapshot , version 1. Privacy A snapshot contains your bookmark titles, addresses and tags in plain text. Treat it like any personal document and review it before you share it. Restore a snapshot Choose the file Library tools &rarr; Restore snapshot , then pick a snapshot JSON (up to 10 MiB). Read the preview The dialog reports Folders / bookmarks / excluded , shows the tree, and lists anything excluded because its address is not http / https . Nothing has been created yet. Technical details are available as an optional disclosure. Choose a destination Pick a writable folder. Restoration is additive : it creates a new folder named Bookmark Scope snapshot YYYY-MM-DD inside it and rebuilds the tree there. Existing bookmarks are never overwritten, merged or deduplicated. Restore Choose Restore into new folder . The result reads Created / excluded / failed / remaining , followed by a result for each item. What a restore cannot do Original IDs and original dates added are not restored. Chrome assigns them. Dates in the snapshot are descriptive only. It does not restore Chrome&rsquo;s own root folders as roots. They become ordinary folders inside the new snapshot folder. It does not restore saved views, history, settings or health results. A folder that fails to be created leaves its children not attempted . Nothing is retried automatically. Running the same restore twice makes a second copy. Check the result and your bookmarks before retrying. The recovery journal While restoring, Bookmark Scope records each created item in a journal in its local storage, so a partial or interrupted restore leaves evidence. The Recovery journal button downloads it as bookmark-scope-restore-journal.json . The twenty most recent completed restores are kept; incomplete ones are kept until you resolve them. Creation of a bookmark and writing the journal are separate browser operations, so a crash between the two can leave a bookmark that is not journaled. Import # Choose More actions &rarr; Import bookmarks&hellip; (list header) or Library tools &rarr; Import bookmarks&hellip; , then pick a file of up to 10 MB . The picker offers .html , .json , .csv and .txt . Supported formats Source File How it is recognised What is imported Pocket HTML export File name contains pocket or ril_export , or the content starts like a Pocket list Title, address, added time, tags Pinboard JSON export File name contains pinboard and either ends in .json or the content starts with [ Title (Pinboard&rsquo;s &ldquo;description&rdquo;), address, time, tags Raindrop.io CSV export File name contains raindrop , or the header starts id,title, Title, address, created time, tags Bookmark Scope JSON export (array or an object with a bookmarks array) File name ends in .json Title, address, path, date, tags Bookmark Scope CSV export ( Title,URL,Folder path,Date added,Tags,Text encoding ) File name ends in .csv Same fields Plain text One address per line Any other file Addresses only; titles become Imported 1 , Imported 2 &hellip; Browser bookmark files A bookmarks.html exported by Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari goes through the same preview as every other format. Bookmarks are created flat in the folder you choose; the file&rsquo;s folders show only as a path on each row (for example Bookmarks bar / News ) and are not recreated, and the added date is not set. To rebuild a folder tree, use a Bookmark Scope snapshot. Bookmarks that are not http or https (bookmarklets, place: and about: entries) are excluded, and a warning counts them. Embedded icons in the file are ignored. Firefox tags are kept; Chrome files have none. The import preview Nothing is created until you confirm. The dialog shows how many bookmarks the file contains, how many match your existing library or repeat within the file, and how many were excluded (invalid or not http / https ), plus any parser warnings. You choose: Destination : a writable folder. All imported bookmarks are created flat in that folder; folder paths and original dates in the file are descriptive only. Duplicate policy : Skip matches (default): do not create bookmarks that match an existing bookmark or an earlier row in the same file. Keep all : create every supported row. Merge tags : add the row&rsquo;s tags to the first existing match instead of creating a copy; the existing bookmark&rsquo;s title, address and folder are not changed. Choose Import bookmarks to apply. The library is re-checked at that moment. The result reads Created / merged / skipped / failed / remaining with a line per item. Safety rules for imports Only http and https addresses are imported; anything else is excluded and counted. If saving a bookmark&rsquo;s tags fails, Bookmark Scope tries to remove that one new bookmark so you do not get a half-imported item, and records what it could and could not do. A partial import is journaled. If the dashboard finds an unfinished import on startup it shows an Import recovery toast with a JSON button to download the evidence. The Recovery journal button in the import dialog does the same. Export visible (JSON or CSV) # In the list header menu, choose Export visible . You get bookmark-manager-visible-<timestamp>.json containing the bookmarks that match the current search and filters: title, address, folder path, date added and tags. It does not include IDs, preferences, history or health results. This JSON can be fed back into Import bookmarks&h"},{"t":"Command palette and keyboard shortcuts","u":"guides/command-palette.html","d":"The complete list of Bookmark Scope keyboard shortcuts and the searchable command palette, verified against the source.","h":["The command palette","Keyboard shortcuts","Popup","In the settings page","Accessibility notes"],"x":"The command palette # In the dashboard, press Ctrl + Shift + P ( Cmd + Shift + P on Mac). A dialog titled Command palette opens with a Search commands box. Type to filter, use the arrow keys to move, press Enter to run. Type to filter by name. Matching ignores case and looks anywhere in the command name. &darr; and &uarr; move the highlight, and wrap around. Enter runs the highlighted command. Esc closes the palette without running anything. Tab stays inside the dialog. When it closes, focus returns to where you were. If another dialog is already open, the shortcut does nothing, so you cannot stack dialogs by accident. The shortcut was chosen to avoid Ctrl + K , which browsers use for search. The thirteen commands Commands that change bookmarks keep their normal confirmations; the palette is only a faster way to reach them. Command Library tools group What it does Saved views Organize Save, open or delete a smart view Bulk tags Organize Add, remove, rename or merge tags with preview and undo. See Organizing Duplicate preview Organize Choose survivors and merge duplicates. See Cleaning up Delete selected Organize Delete the selected bookmarks (asks first) Resumable scan Check links Start, pause, resume or cancel a saved link scan. See Link health Inspect visible Check links Check the selected, or else all visible, bookmarks once Repair redirected Check links Update redirected bookmarks to their final address (asks first) Export snapshot Backup and import Download a snapshot file. See Backup and restore Restore snapshot Backup and import Choose a snapshot file and restore it into a new folder Import bookmarks&hellip; Backup and import Choose a file to import, with preview Export visible as CSV Backup and import Download the visible bookmarks as a spreadsheet-safe CSV file. See Backup and restore Diagnostic export Troubleshoot Preview and download a privacy-filtered diagnostic file. See Diagnostics Clear cached health data Troubleshoot Forget cached link-health results (asks first) The same commands appear under the Library tools button, grouped as shown. The palette is the quickest route to them from the keyboard. Keyboard shortcuts # Dashboard Dashboard keyboard shortcuts Keys Does this Notes Ctrl + Shift + P / Cmd + Shift + P Open the command palette Works even when a text box has focus; ignored while another dialog is open / Focus the search box Only when you are not already typing in a field Ctrl + A / Cmd + A Select all bookmarks in the current view Selects the whole filtered view, not only drawn rows &darr; / &uarr; Move the active row down or up The detail panel follows the active row Enter Open the active bookmark in a new tab E Edit the active bookmark Then Ctrl + Enter saves and Esc cancels Space Select or unselect the active row Delete / Backspace Delete the selected bookmarks Only if something is selected; asks to confirm Esc Back out one layer See below 1 2 3 Page, Host, Domain scope Only when the dashboard was opened from a right-click entry; see below The 1, 2 and 3 keys switch the dashboard between the page, host and domain scope of an address. They need an address to work from, which only a right-click entry (see the popup guide ) provides. On a dashboard you opened normally they show This page is not supported with an empty list; reload the dashboard to get your whole library back. When single-key shortcuts are paused While you are typing in the search box, a title field or a tag field. (The exceptions are Ctrl + Shift + P , Esc to cancel an edit, and Ctrl + Enter to save one.) While focus is on a button, link, checkbox, menu item or other control, so Space and Enter keep their normal meaning on it. While any dialog (confirm, Library tools, palette) is open. Escape clears things one layer at a time With the cursor outside any text box, each press of Esc undoes the innermost thing that is active. In order: Cancel editing a bookmark. Clear the search text. Clear the active filter chip. Clear the selection. Dismiss the toast (which also removes an Undo button you have not used). Menus close on Esc first. Inside any dialog, Esc closes the dialog. Tag field on a row Enter or , adds the tag and Esc clears the field. See Organizing . Popup # The popup has no custom shortcuts. Esc closes its menu or dialog, and Tab moves between controls. Chrome lets you assign a shortcut to open the popup at chrome://extensions/shortcuts . In the settings page # Standard form behavior: Tab between fields, Enter in a field submits Save settings . Accessibility notes # Dialogs have accessible names, trap Tab while open, close on Esc or a backdrop click, and return focus to the control that opened them. Each row checkbox is named with the bookmark&rsquo;s title and address. Screen-reader behavior and contrast have not been formally audited. If you find a barrier, please report it ."},{"t":"Settings reference","u":"guides/settings-reference.html","d":"Every Bookmark Scope setting, its choices and default, plus the preferences that are remembered automatically.","h":["Behavior defaults","Appearance and language","Settings that are not on this page","Where settings are stored"],"x":"The settings page. Choose Save settings to apply. Press Save settings to apply changes. Reset defaults restores the nine preferences marked below, and nothing else. Behavior defaults # Setting Choices Default Effect Default mode This page, This host, This domain This domain The popup&rsquo;s starting scope, and the scope the toolbar badge counts. Resets to this on Reset defaults . Default sort Title, URL, Newest, Oldest, Folder path Title The popup&rsquo;s starting sort order. Folder path is available here only. Popup width Compact, Comfortable Comfortable The popup&rsquo;s width: Compact is 380 px, Comfortable is 420 px. Merge strategy Keep newest bookmark, Keep oldest bookmark Keep newest bookmark Which copy is pre-selected as the survivor in Duplicate preview . You can still change each group. Appearance and language # Setting Choices Default Effect Theme System, Light, Dark System System follows your browser or operating-system colour scheme and updates live. Color palette Teal, Blue, Indigo, Neutral Teal The accent colour of the popup, dashboard and settings. Language Auto (browser) plus 52 languages Auto (browser) Auto uses your browser language when supported and falls back to English. See Appearance, themes and languages . URL matching rules Setting Default Effect Ignore query string in page matching Off When on, ?utm_source=x and other query strings do not make two pages different. Affects duplicate detection, Page scope, the badge and link-health keys. Ignore hash fragment in page matching On When on, #section does not make two pages different. Examples are in Finding and searching . Changing these options changes which bookmarks count as duplicates immediately, so review before you delete. Scheduled health scan Setting Default Range Effect Enable scheduled background scan Off On / off Checks links in the background and notifies you about newly broken ones. Turning it on asks for site access; if you decline, it stays off and the page says Background scan needs site-access permission. It was not enabled. Scan interval (days) 7 1&ndash;90 Time between runs. A value outside the range is corrected when you save. A status line shows Background scan is off. , Background scan is on. First scan will run shortly. or Last scan: &hellip;. N bookmarks currently look broken. How it works: Link health . Note that Reset defaults does not change this section. Review reminders Setting Default Range Effect Enable review reminders Off On / off Shows a review is due notification on a schedule. Notifications only; no site access is needed. Reminder interval (days) 14 1&ndash;365 Time between reminders. How the schedule moves is explained in Review reminders . Reset defaults does not change this section either. Settings that are not on this page # These are remembered automatically as you use the product, each by the screen you change it on: Preference Where you change it Default Popup scope (Page / Host / Domain) Popup From Default mode Popup sort Popup &rarr; More controls From Default sort Theme and colour palette Popup or dashboard menus, or Settings System, Teal Dashboard filter chip Dashboard All Grouping and group order Dashboard toolbar Flat view, Default order Side panel collapsed Dashboard side rail Collapsed Dashboard sort Dashboard toolbar ( Sort visible bookmarks ) Title A&ndash;Z. Independent of the popup sort Density (compact) Dashboard toolbar Normal. Not saved between visits Quick-setup card dismissed Got it on the card Shown on first run Where settings are stored # Everything above is stored in this browser profile&rsquo;s extension storage ( storage.local ), not synced across devices. Privacy, permissions and data lists every kind of data."},{"t":"Appearance, themes, palettes and languages","u":"guides/appearance-and-languages.html","d":"Light, dark and system themes, four colour palettes, 52 interface languages including right-to-left layouts, and how translation coverage works.","h":["Theme","Colour palette","Languages","Browser metadata","Reading these guides"],"x":"Theme # Choose System , Light or Dark . Theme buttons appear in the popup&rsquo;s More controls menu and the dashboard&rsquo;s More actions menu, and the settings page has a Theme drop-down. System follows the colour scheme of your browser or operating system and changes live when it does. The same dashboard in light and dark. Colour palette # Four accent palettes: Teal (default), Blue , Indigo and Neutral . The palette applies to the popup, the dashboard and settings, and a change in one open window updates the others. Languages # Bookmark Scope ships 52 interface languages : English plus 51 others. All language files are packaged inside the extension; choosing a language never downloads anything from a website. Choose a language under Settings &rarr; Language . Auto (browser) matches your browser language, including regional variants (for example es-MX uses Spanish), and falls back to English if the language is not supported. The list shows each language in its own script followed by its English name. The 52 interface languages Languages Amharic, Arabic, Bengali, Bulgarian, Catalan, Chinese (Simplified), Chinese (Traditional), Croatian, Czech, Danish, Dutch, English, Estonian, Filipino, Finnish, French, Gujarati, German, Greek, Hebrew, Hindi, Hungarian, Indonesian, Italian, Japanese, Kannada, Korean, Latvian, Lithuanian, Malay, Malayalam, Marathi, Norwegian, Persian, Polish, Portuguese (Brazil), Portuguese (Portugal), Romanian, Russian, Serbian, Slovak, Slovenian, Spanish, Swahili, Swedish, Tamil, Telugu, Thai, Turkish, Ukrainian, Urdu, Vietnamese Right-to-left languages Arabic, Persian, Hebrew and Urdu are laid out right-to-left. When one is active, the page direction flips and menus, panels and dialogs follow it. Bookmark titles and addresses use automatic text direction per item, so a Hebrew title next to an English URL both read correctly. The dashboard with a right-to-left language in the dark theme. How complete are the translations? Most interface strings (the popup, dashboard, settings, notifications) are translated in all 52 languages. A string with no translation appears in English. Some newer strings, notably in the Library tools dialogs and the newest Settings options, are still being translated. Until they are reviewed they appear in English in languages other than English and Persian. Translations beyond English and Persian have not been reviewed by native speakers, so wording may be imperfect. Right-to-left layouts have been checked in the dashboard dialogs, but not exhaustively on every screen. In an earlier narrow-width Persian capture the row of filter chips looked clipped at one edge. Browser metadata # The extension&rsquo;s name and description in chrome://extensions come from separate packaged files, one per language, and follow your browser&rsquo;s display language. Reading these guides # This site follows your system colour scheme and has its own Dark mode / Light mode button in the header."},{"t":"Privacy, permissions and data","u":"guides/privacy-and-data.html","d":"Which permissions Bookmark Scope asks for and why, what it stores on your device, and the only cases in which anything leaves your browser.","h":["The short version","Permissions, and why each is there","What is stored on your device","What can leave your browser","Sharing files safely","Controlling access"],"x":"The short version # Everything Bookmark Scope knows is stored in your browser profile. Nothing is sent to the developer. The extension&rsquo;s own code contains exactly one kind of outgoing request to arbitrary sites: the link-health check of the addresses in your bookmarks. It needs a permission you grant. Your bookmarks stay in Chrome&rsquo;s bookmark store. Bookmark Scope reads and edits them through Chrome&rsquo;s bookmarks API, and keeps tags and other extras separately. Permissions, and why each is there # These come from the extension&rsquo;s manifest. Chrome&rsquo;s install prompt may group or reword them. Permission What Bookmark Scope uses it for bookmarks Read your bookmark tree; create, edit, move and delete bookmarks when you ask it to tabs Read the address and title of the active tab (for scope matching and the star button) and open tabs storage Keep settings, tags, saved views, history, caches and journals on this device alarms Wake the background worker for the optional scheduled link check and review reminders notifications Show the three notifications listed in Review reminders and notifications contextMenus Add the three right-click entries clipboardWrite Copy the addresses of selected bookmarks when you press Copy URLs or Copy favicon Ask Chrome for the small site icons it already has, so rows can show them Optional: http://*/* and https://*/* Contact bookmarked sites to check whether they still load. Not granted at install. Chrome asks when you first start a check or enable the scheduled scan. The extension declares no content scripts, so it does not run inside the web pages you visit, and it does not read page content. What is stored on your device # All of this lives in the extension&rsquo;s local storage for the current browser profile. It is not synced to your Google account and not shared between devices. Data What it contains Limit / retention Preferences Your settings, dashboard filter, grouping, theme, language Small Tags A map from bookmark ID to its tags 32 characters per tag, 20 tags per bookmark. Removed when the bookmark is deleted Link-health cache Per page address: status, time, status code, final address, error text Ignored after 7 days; at most 5,000 results Manual scan job The list of addresses in your saved scan and its progress One job; up to 50,000 addresses Scheduled-scan state On/off, interval, last run, broken count, and the last address it attempted Small Saved views Search text, filters, grouping, tag filter Up to 50 Activity history Counts, times and short notes about actions Bookmark Scope took Latest 60 entries Import and restore journals The bookmarks created by an import or restore and their outcomes Latest 20 completed, plus any incomplete ones. Contains your bookmark addresses and titles Diagnostic events Technical event names and timestamps. A few events can include a bookmark address in this local store Latest 200 (info, warning, error) The extension does not use browser sync storage. Clearing the link-health cache, resetting defaults and uninstalling are covered in Installation . What can leave your browser # Link-health requests (only with your permission) A check sends a HEAD request, or a GET if the site refuses HEAD , directly from your browser to the bookmarked address. The site sees an ordinary request from your computer. Bookmark Scope receives the response status and final address, and nothing else. No proxy, relay or developer server is involved. It happens only if you start a check or enable the scheduled scan. Links you choose to click The footer and About screens contain links to the GitHub repository, a support page and a store review page. They open in a new tab only when you click. Chrome&rsquo;s own extension update checks These are performed by Chrome, not by Bookmark Scope. Everything else, including searching, tagging, scanning for duplicates, snapshots and translations, runs locally. The language files are packaged with the extension and read from it, not downloaded. How this was checked This statement comes from reading the extension&rsquo;s JavaScript for network calls: the only fetch to outside addresses is the health check, and the only other fetch reads packaged language files. It is not a network traffic capture or an independent security audit. Sharing files safely # File Contains your bookmarks? Snapshot ( bookmark-scope-snapshot.json ) Yes : titles, addresses, folders, tags Restore or import recovery journal Yes Export visible ( bookmark-manager-visible-&hellip;.json or .csv ) Yes Diagnostic export No . See Diagnostics Controlling access # Decline the site-access prompt and link checks simply do not run. Everything else works. Revoke site access later from the extension&rsquo;s Details page in chrome://extensions . Remove the extension to delete its stored data."},{"t":"Diagnostics export","u":"guides/diagnostics.html","d":"Download a privacy-filtered diagnostic file to attach to a bug report. See exactly what is in it before you save it.","h":["Create one","What is in it","What is left out","Where to send it"],"x":"Create one # Open it In the dashboard choose Library tools &rarr; Diagnostic export (or find Diagnostic export in the command palette). Read it The dialog shows the exact text that would be saved. Nothing has been downloaded yet. Download Choose Download . Your browser saves bookmark-scope-diagnostics.json . Nothing is uploaded anywhere; sending it to anyone is up to you. What is in it # { \"format\": \"bookmark-scope-diagnostics\", \"version\": 1, \"extensionVersion\": \"5.0.0\", \"createdAt\": \"2026-10-07T09:30:00.000Z\", \"redaction\": \"allowlisted-fields-only\", \"events\": [ { \"ts\": \"2026-10-07T09:29:51.120Z\", \"scope\": \"dashboard\", \"event\": \"init_started\", \"level\": \"info\", \"metrics\": {} } ] } The extension version and the time of export. Up to 200 recent events, each with a time, a scope ( background , dashboard , popup , options or app ), an event name from a fixed list, a level, and a few numeric or true/false counters such as count , failed , skipped or total . What is left out # The filter works on an allowlist: anything it does not recognise is dropped or replaced with other . In particular the file never includes bookmark addresses or titles, bookmark IDs, tag names, free-form error messages, or any nested data. The dialog repeats this: Includes event types, timestamps and numeric counts. Excludes bookmark URLs, titles, IDs, tags and error text. Be careful This protection applies only to the diagnostic export. Snapshots, recovery journals and Export visible files do contain your bookmarks. Do not attach those to a public bug report. Where to send it # Open an issue on the GitHub repository and describe what you did, what you expected and what happened. Mention your browser and version. Read the file once yourself first."},{"t":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","u":"guides/troubleshooting-faq.html","d":"Answers to common problems with Bookmark Scope: missing results, permission prompts, scans that stop, Undo, tags, languages and more.","h":["Using the popup","Searching and filters","Link health and permissions","Deleting, Undo and recovery","Tags","Import, export and restore","Language and appearance","When nothing helps","FAQ"],"x":"Using the popup # The popup says &ldquo;Unsupported page&rdquo; or &ldquo;No scope&rdquo;. You are on a page that is not http or https (for example chrome:// , a new tab or a local file). Open a normal website. A bookmark I know I saved is not listed. Check the scope switch: Page only matches the exact page address. Widen to Host or Domain . Also clear the search box, and remember the list shows only the first eight rows. The star is not filled, but I bookmarked this page. The star compares the exact address of the tab with your bookmarks. A bookmark of the same page with a different query string, fragment or trailing slash is not the same string. Search for the site in the popup instead. The toolbar badge number differs from what I expected. The badge follows your default scope (Page, Host or Domain) and the URL-matching options in Settings . Searching and filters # The dashboard shows nothing for my search. Dashboard search is exact substring matching with AND logic, in the title, address and folder only. Check for an active filter chip or tag chip too; press Esc to clear layers. The popup, not the dashboard, has fuzzy fallback. Searching tag:work does nothing. There is no tag operator. Click a tag chip in the Tags strip above the list. health:broken finds nothing. That filter matches only bookmarks with a recorded result. Run a check first, and note results expire after 7 days. Two bookmarks are marked duplicates that I consider different. They resolve to the same page address under your matching options. If a query string is the only difference, make sure Ignore query string is off. Two obvious duplicates are not flagged. Differences in scheme ( http vs https ), a www. prefix, a non-default port, letter case in the path, or the query string make them different pages. See the examples . Link health and permissions # &ldquo;Health scans need temporary site access. Permission denied&rdquo;. You declined Chrome&rsquo;s site-access prompt. Start the check again and accept, or grant access from the extension&rsquo;s Details page. Lots of &ldquo;Broken 403&rdquo; / &ldquo;401&rdquo; / &ldquo;429&rdquo;. Those statuses mean the site refused an automated request. Open a few in your browser before drawing conclusions. A bookmark shows as Redirected but the page looks the same. The site really answered with a redirect, for example from http to https or to a new path. A different #fragment or letter case in the host alone is not counted. Check Redirects to in the detail panel before you repair; a redirect to a sign-in or consent page is not a fix. See Repairing redirects . My scan stopped when I closed the tab. Manual scans run only while the dashboard is open. Reopen Library tools and choose Resume scan . Use the scheduled scan for hands-free checking. The scheduled scan did not run or did not notify me. It must be enabled, site access must be granted, and Chrome must be running when it is due. It notifies you only when bookmarks have newly turned broken since the last result. Check the status line in Settings. I enabled the scan but the checkbox turned itself off. Saving asks for site access; declining switches it off again and shows Background scan needs site-access permission. It was not enabled. Deleting, Undo and recovery # Undo has gone. Undo exists only while the toast is on screen and the page that created it stays open. Pressing Esc , starting another action that shows a toast, reloading or closing the tab ends it. Restore from a snapshot instead. After Undo the bookmark looks new, or is no longer &ldquo;old&rdquo;. Chrome gives restored bookmarks a new ID and a new date added. That cannot be changed. The toast says &ldquo;Deleted: 3. Failed: 1.&rdquo; One bookmark could not be removed (for example it was already gone or protected). Undo covers only the three that were deleted. The duplicate merge stopped with &ldquo;Preview changed; reopen it before merging&rdquo;. A bookmark in the preview was edited, moved or deleted, or its tags changed, after you opened it. Reopen Duplicate preview . The merge says the tag limit was exceeded. The union of tags would exceed 20 on the survivor. Remove a few tags from one copy, then try again. Tags # My tags are gone after reinstalling or on another computer. Tags live in the extension&rsquo;s local storage, which uninstalling deletes and which is not synced. Export a snapshot before uninstalling; a snapshot restore recreates tags on the restored bookmarks. Tag text changed case or lost spaces. Tags are normalised to lower case with single spaces and a limit of 32 characters. I can&rsquo;t add a tag from the row. Click the row first so it becomes active; the + add tag box appears only on the active row. Press Enter or comma to add. For many bookmarks at once use Bulk tags ; see Organizing . Bulk tags affected too many (or too few) bookmarks. With nothing selected it targets all visible bookmarks. Read the count in the dialog before applying, and use Undo tag changes if needed. Import, export and restore # The import preview shows zero supported bookmarks. The file had no http / https addresses the importer understands, or its format was not recognised. Browser bookmarks.html files are supported; check that the file really is a bookmarks export (it should contain folders and links) and that its addresses are http or https , because bookmarklets, place: and about: entries are excluded. See Backup and restore . &ldquo;Import file is too large (max 10 MB).&rdquo; Split the file into smaller ones. My restore created a second copy. Restore is additive and never deduplicates; each run creates a new folder. Delete the extra folder in Chrome&rsquo;s bookmark manager. A restore or import stopped half way. Download the Recovery journal , look at what was created in Chrome&rsquo;s bookmark manager, and only then decide whether to retry. Language and appearance # Some dialogs are in English though I picked another language. Some newer strings fall back to English until they are translated and reviewed. See Appearance and languages . The popup is the wrong width. Choose Popup width under Settings &rarr; Behavior defaults: Compact is 380 px"},{"t":"Limits and known limitations","u":"guides/limits.html","d":"An honest list of what Bookmark Scope cannot do, hard limits, and behaviors that were found to be wrong or unfinished when this guide was written.","h":["How to read this page","Hard limits and design boundaries","Not verified in a real browser","","Import and export","Languages","Reporting problems"],"x":"How to read this page # These limits were checked against the source and, where noted, by running the extension in a disposable Chromium profile with synthetic bookmarks. They may change in a later release. Hard limits and design boundaries # Area Limit Browsers Chrome/Chromium only has been checked. Firefox and other browsers are not verified. Pages Only http and https addresses are matched, checked or imported. Folders Bookmark Scope cannot create, rename or delete folders (except the single new folder a snapshot restore creates). Use Chrome&rsquo;s bookmark manager. Undo Delete and move Undo recreate or reposition bookmarks but cannot restore original IDs or dates added. Undo exists only for the open page. Redirect repair has no Undo. Atomicity Operations that touch many bookmarks are not all-or-nothing. A partial result is reported and recovered item by item. Tags 32 characters each, 20 per bookmark. Stored only on this browser profile, not synced, not part of Chrome&rsquo;s bookmark data. Old threshold Fixed at 730 days. Link-health timeouts 8 seconds per request, 5 for the GET fallback. Health cache 7 days; 5,000 results. Scheduled scan Up to 250 addresses per run, interval 1&ndash;90 days; runs only while the browser is running. Resumable scan Between 1 and 50,000 addresses; one saved job; runs only while the dashboard is open. Import / restore size 10 MB for import files, 10 MiB for snapshots; snapshots up to 50,000 items and 100 folder levels. Saved views At most 50; names up to 80 characters. Journals and history 20 recent completed journals, 60 activity entries, 200 diagnostic events. Popup list Shows 8 rows. Search Substring matching; no quoted phrases, no tag operator, no regular expressions. Fuzzy matching only in the popup. Cross-device No sync of Bookmark Scope data. Not verified in a real browser # Chrome&rsquo;s actual permission prompt for site access, and live link checks against real websites (the project&rsquo;s automated tests use controlled replacements). Screen-reader output, measured colour contrast and zoom behavior. Drag and drop with a touch screen. Translation quality in languages other than English and Persian. Behavior after the browser is killed mid-operation (a crash between creating a bookmark and writing its journal can leave an unjournaled bookmark). Scope shortcuts and the right-click menu The right-click entries open a dashboard that is already narrowed to one address&rsquo;s domain or page. There is no on-screen control to change that scope; the keys 1 , 2 and 3 do, and only in a dashboard opened that way. On a dashboard opened normally, those keys show This page is not supported and an empty list. Reload to recover. Link health Any 4xx is &ldquo;Broken&rdquo; , including 401, 403 and 429. Treat the status as a hint. A true redirect is not always a better address. A redirect to a sign-in page, a consent page or a homepage still counts as Redirected. Repair redirected has no Undo, so read Redirects to first. The check judges the response headers only. A page that loads but shows an error message, a login wall or a consent page is still &ldquo;Healthy&rdquo;. Skipped: Chrome Web Store addresses and non-web schemes. The prompt for site access calls it &ldquo;temporary&rdquo;, but Bookmark Scope does not revoke it afterwards. Import and export # Browser bookmarks.html files (Netscape format: Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari, Brave and most bookmark managers) are imported as a flat list. Each row shows its folder path, but the folders are not recreated, and the added date and the icon are not imported. Addresses that are not http or https (bookmarklets, place: , about: , file: ) are excluded. At most 50,000 bookmarks and 100 folder levels are read from a file of up to 10 MB. The reader is a small tolerant tokenizer, not a full HTML engine, and was checked with synthetic Chrome-style and Firefox-style files rather than real exports from every browser. Imports are flat: folder paths and added dates in the file are descriptive only. Restores are additive and are not deduplicated; repeating one creates a second copy. The HTML reader for Pocket exports is a lightweight parser, not a full HTML engine. The dashboard exports the visible list only, as JSON or as CSV; there is no export of just the selected bookmarks. The CSV export has been checked against this project&rsquo;s own importer and in a real Chromium download, but has not been opened in Excel, Numbers or Google Sheets. It starts with a UTF-8 byte-order mark so that spreadsheets read non-Latin text. A cell that could be read as a formula begins with an apostrophe; a spreadsheet may show that apostrophe, and Bookmark Scope removes it again on import. Languages # Some newer strings, including parts of the Library tools dialogs and the newest Settings options, appear in English until their translations are reviewed. Translations for other languages have not had native-speaker review. Reporting problems # Open an issue on GitHub . A diagnostic export is safe to attach; snapshots and journals are not."},{"t":"All guides","u":"guides/index.html","d":"The full list of Bookmark Scope user guides.","h":["Get started","Everyday use","Link health","Saved work","Reference","Trust and support"],"x":"Quick start and first run Installation, updates and removal The popup: scope, search and quick actions Dashboard tour Finding and searching Organizing: tags, folders and moving Cleaning up: duplicates, untitled, old and collisions Link health: checking, scanning and repairing Review reminders and notifications Smart views Backup, restore, import and export Command palette and keyboard shortcuts Settings reference Appearance, themes, palettes and languages Privacy, permissions and data Diagnostics export Troubleshooting and FAQ Limits and known limitations"},{"t":"Bookmark Scope user guide","u":"index.html","d":"Overview of the Bookmark Scope user guide.","h":["Get started","Everyday use","Stay safe"],"x":"overview home start"}];
